        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Warsaw (1920)</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">August 1920: the final Soviet assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> began on 12 August and the Polish counterattack from the south on 16 August; organised Soviet resistance had ceased by 21 August</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and the country around it, including Radzymin, the Modlin Fortress, the Wkra River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>–Wieprz area to the south, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, with 32 divisions, 46,000 infantry and 2,000 cavalry in the Polish order of battle; Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Western Front under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a>, with 24 divisions in four armies</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Polish counterattack disrupted the Soviet offensive and forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> into a disorganised withdrawal eastward. Three of the four Soviet armies of the North-Western Front all but disintegrated.</span></div>
