    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a> drew up his plan on the night of 5 to 6 August 1920 and gathered a Task Force of about 20,000 men in the south, a plan many senior officers and French advisers called amateurish and Piłsudski himself called a gamble.</li><li>On the first day of the counteroffensive, 16 August, four of five Polish divisions advanced 45 kilometres north without opposition into the gap between the two Soviet fronts.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> losses are put at about 15,000 dead, 500 missing, 10,000 wounded and 65,000 captured, against Polish losses of approximately 4,500 killed, 22,000 wounded and 10,000 missing.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>To stop the one Soviet radio station of the 4th Army that was still working, Polish operators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> recited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> in Polish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> on its frequency, so that it lost contact with its headquarters.</p></aside>
    </section>

