    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">June 1920</span><span class="rf-text">Semyon Budyonny's 1st Cavalry Army breaks through the Polish lines and all Polish fronts collapse</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1920</span><span class="rf-text">Tukhachevsky's Western Front begins an all-out assault in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and reaches Białystok on 28 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1920</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a> conceives a revised plan at the Belweder Palace on the night of 5 to 6 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1920</span><span class="rf-text">The final Soviet assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> begins on 12 August with the attack on the Praga bridgehead and at Radzymin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1920</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a>'s Task Force begins its march north from the Wieprz River on 16 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1920</span><span class="rf-text">The Polish army defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> again at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Niemen</a> River, fought from 15 to 21 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1920</span><span class="rf-text">A ceasefire is signed on 12 October under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the fighting ends by 18 October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1921</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Treaty of Riga</a>, signed on 18 March, ends hostilities</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-warsaw-1920" data-v="32437aeedc" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Red Army's final assault on Warsaw</b> The Soviet 16th Army attacked at Radzymin, east of Warsaw, while the 3rd and 15th Armies came up to Modlin. The Polish 5th Army had to face three Soviet armies at once.</li><li><b>Budyonny's army marches on Lwów, not Warsaw</b> The Soviet High Command ordered the 1st Cavalry Army to advance on Warsaw from the south. It marched on Lwów instead and missed the battle, leaving only a weak Mazyr Group on the southern flank.</li><li><b>The Polish 5th Army counterattacks in the north</b> The 5th Army crossed the Wkra and fought the Soviet 3rd and 15th Armies at Nasielsk. The Soviet advance towards Warsaw and Modlin was halted at the end of 15 August.</li><li><b>The Assault Group breaks through the Mazyr Group</b> Piłsudski's Assault Group marched north from the Wieprz and met almost no opposition. It routed the Mazyr Group and cut the supply lines of the Soviet 16th Army.</li><li><b>The Soviet 16th Army is cut off near Białystok</b> Tukhachevsky ordered a retreat, but his orders came too late or not at all. The Polish 1st Legions Division made a forced march and cut off the 16th Army, and most of its troops surrendered.</li><li><b>The northern Soviet armies join the general rout</b> The 15th Army, which had tried to cover the retreat of the 4th Army, was defeated twice and joined the rout. By 21 August all organised Soviet resistance had ceased.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski.jpg" alt="Józef Piłsudski" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%B3zef_Pi%C5%82sudski_(-1930).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> regained its independence after the partitions of 1795 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, fought to keep it and to shape the borders of a new multinational federation, called Intermarium, from the territories of its former partitioners <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. In 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> gained the upper hand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. Lenin viewed Poland as a bridge to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> to central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Bolshevik speeches asserted that the revolution would be carried west on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonets</a> of Russian soldiers and that the shortest route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> lay through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>, the Polish head of state, formed an alliance with the Ukrainian leader Symon Petlyura on 21 April 1920, and their forces pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> on 7 May. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> counter-offensive then nullified the Polish Kiev Operation. Semyon Budyonny's 1st Cavalry Army broke through the Polish lines in early June, and all Polish fronts collapsed.</p><p>On 4 July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-tukhachevsky">Mikhail Tukhachevsky</a>'s Western Front began an all-out assault in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> from the Berezina River. The Red Army seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> on 19 July, captured the Brześć Fortress on 22 July and reached Białystok on 28 July. Polish and Soviet delegations met at Baranavichy in early August, but the talks came to nothing. By mid-1920 foreign observers expected Poland to collapse at any moment. The Bolsheviks expected the fall of Warsaw to spark communist uprisings and to clear the way for the Red Army to join the German Revolution.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Warsaw (1920)&#8221; cites where it covers Prelude:</p><ul><li>Isaac Babel, 1920 Diary (Yale University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich.jpg" alt="Ivan Paskevich" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%98%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%A4%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87_%D0%9F%D0%B0%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Polish order of battle lists three fronts, seven armies and 32 divisions: 46,000 infantry, 2,000 cavalry, 730 machine guns, 192 artillery batteries and several units of tanks, mostly FT-17. The army was newly created and its rifles and artillery were made in at least six countries, each using different ammunition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a> remarked that in the 21st Division almost half the soldiers who paraded before him were barefoot.</p><p>Piłsudski's plan, worked out on the night of 5 to 6 August, called for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> to withdraw across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and hold the bridgeheads at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and on the Wieprz. The 1st and 2nd Armies were to hold their entrenched positions in front of Warsaw at all costs, and the 5th Army under General Władysław Sikorski was to defend the Modlin area and strike when it could. The crucial role went to a newly formed Task Force of about 20,000 men under Piłsudski, drawn from the best units of the southern front and reinforced by the 4th and 3rd Armies. It was to strike north from the Vistula–Wieprz triangle at a weak spot between the Soviet Western and Southwestern Fronts, held only by the weak Mozyr Group, and meet the 5th Army near the East Prussian border.</p><p>Tukhachevsky planned to cross the Vistula near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek">Włocławek</a>, northwest of Warsaw, and attack the capital from that direction, repeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a>'s manoeuvre of 1831 and cutting the Poles off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, the only port open to arms and supplies. His weakness was the southern flank, covered only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripet-marshes">Pinsk Marshes</a> and the Mozyr Group, whose 57th Infantry Division had 8,000 men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The final Soviet assault began on 12 August. The 16th Army attacked at Radzymin, 23 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and the Praga bridgehead came under frontal assault. Radzymin changed hands several times. The Polish 5th Army had to fight three Soviet armies at once and its lines were broken, but around midnight the 203rd Uhlan Regiment broke through and destroyed a radio station of the Soviet 4th Army. Polish operators jammed the remaining one, and the army marched on toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> and Płock, unaware of Tukhachevsky's order to turn south. The 5th Army counterattacked across the Wkra River on 14 August. On 15 August the Soviet advance was halted and Radzymin was retaken. The closest any Soviet unit came was Izabelin, 13 kilometres northwest of the capital.</p><p>Budyonny's 1st Cavalry Army disobeyed the order to advance on Warsaw from the south and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a> instead. On 16 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a>'s Task Force marched north from the Wieprz against the Mozyr Group, which had been reduced to one or two divisions on a 150-kilometre front. Only one of five Polish divisions met any opposition, and four advanced 45 kilometres unopposed, cutting the supply lines of the Soviet 16th Army.</p><p>On 18 August Tukhachevsky, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, ordered a retreat, but his orders came too late or not at all. The Polish 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> Infantry Division marched 262 kilometres from Lubartów to Białystok in six days and cut off the 16th Army, most of whose troops surrendered. The 15th Army was defeated on 19 and 20 August, and by 21 August all organised resistance had ceased.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga.jpg" alt="Peace of Riga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peace_treaty_of_Riga_1921.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official document of Poland, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a>'s plan to surround the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> did not succeed completely. Of the four Soviet armies that advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, the 4th, 15th and 16th, with most of Bzhishkyan's 3rd Cavalry Corps, all but disintegrated, their remnants taken prisoner or interned after crossing into German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. The 3rd Army was least affected, since its speed of retreat kept the Polish pursuers from catching it. Between 25,000 and 30,000 Soviet troops reached the German border and, after brief internment, were allowed to leave with their arms and equipment.</p><p>Red Army losses are put at about 15,000 dead, 500 missing, 10,000 wounded and 65,000 captured, or at 10,000 killed, 500 missing, 30,000 wounded and 66,000 taken prisoner. Polish losses are some 4,500 killed, 22,000 wounded and 10,000 missing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> captured about 231 pieces of artillery and 1,023 machine guns. Budyonny's army, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, was beaten at Komarów on 31 August and at Hrubieszów.</p><p>Tukhachevsky reorganised his retreating forces and set up a new defensive line near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a> in September, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> defeated him again at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Niemen</a> River from 15 to 21 September. After the Battle of the Szczara River both sides were exhausted. A ceasefire was signed on 12 October under heavy pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the fighting was over by 18 October, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Treaty of Riga</a> of 18 March 1921 ended hostilities. The Polish victory also forced the cancellation of the Soviet attempt to overthrow the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, planned for August.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Polish plan is known through the memoirs of its makers and the reports written afterwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a> admitted in his memoirs that it was a gamble, and General Maxime Weygand wrote in his that the victory, the plan and the army were Polish. Its authorship was nevertheless disputed. After the battle many reports credited Weygand or the Polish chief of staff Tadeusz Jordan-Rozwadowski, and Paderewski told Allied leaders that Weygand had the idea. According to recent research, the French mission proposed only a minor counter-attack by two divisions towards Mińsk Mazowiecki, Rozwadowski a deeper thrust from the Wieprz, and Piłsudski a large-scale operation. Davies describes the order as issued on the evening of 6 August after Piłsudski and Rozwadowski worked out the details. The Soviets are said to have intercepted a copy of the plan and discarded it as a poor deception.</p><p>On the Soviet side the dispute is over blame. Tukhachevsky blamed Stalin, who as political commissar of the South-Western Front aimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, and Lenin and Trotsky blamed Stalin too. Stalin's secretary Boris Bazhanov argued that the offensive failed because Stalin and his army ignored the commander in chief's orders. In 1973 Thomas Fiddick judged rumours of disobedience by Budyonny or Stalin baseless, but more recent historians say Stalin did disobey. Davies argues that the Soviets were slow to bring forces up and poorly coordinated. Documents found in 2005 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s Central Military Archives show that Polish cryptanalysts had broken Russian ciphers as early as September 1919.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lenin called the result "an enormous defeat" for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>. The Treaty of Riga secured the Polish state's eastern frontiers until 1939. The politician and diplomat Edgar Vincent regards the battle as one of the most important in history, since the Polish victory halted the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> further west. In his view a Soviet victory would have led to a Soviet-established government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and put the Soviets directly on the German border, where they were expecting or had been promised aligned forces.</p><p>The name "Miracle on the Vistula" was coined by the National Democrat deputy Stanisław Stroński to underline his disapproval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a>'s earlier Ukrainian adventure. Prime Minister Wincenty Witos answered that whatever one wrote and whomever one dressed in laurels, this was the 1920 Miracle on the Vistula. Diaries of many participants attribute the outcome to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Blessed Virgin Mary</a>, citing widespread national prayer beforehand and later reports of her appearance on the battlefield. The turn of the battle, 15 August, is the feast of the Assumption, an important date in the Catholic year.</p><p>For many years a myth persisted that the arrival of Allied forces had saved Poland, with Weygand at its centre. The raid of the 203rd Uhlans is sometimes called the Miracle of Ciechanów. Lieutenant Jan Kowalewski, credited with the original breakthrough in breaking Russian ciphers, received the Order of Virtuti Militari in 1921.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In August 1920 Soviet armies under Tukhachevsky reached the approaches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, where a Polish counterattack from the south led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Piłsudski</a> broke their offensive and sent them eastward in disorder. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Treaty of Riga</a> of 1921 then fixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s eastern frontier.</em></p>

